  "xp": 12,
  "structureVariant": "linear",
  "phases": {
    "1": {
      "concept": [
        "Shell sort generalizes insertion sort by first comparing and swapping elements that are far apart (a 'gap'), gradually shrinking the gap down to 1.",
        "This moves far-out-of-place elements toward their correct position faster than plain insertion sort, which only ever compares immediate neighbors.",
        "Unusually for a sorting algorithm, shell sort's complexity isn't a single fixed value — it depends entirely on the gap sequence chosen.",
        "The simplest sequence (halving the gap each pass) gives O(n²) worst case, no better than plain insertion sort, but sequences like Hibbard's or Sedgewick's push it down toward O(n^1.5) or better, which is part of why shell sort is still an active area of analysis rather than a fully solved problem."
      ],
      "analogy": "Combing tangled hair by first working out the big knots with wide strokes, then switching to finer and finer strokes — starting fine on badly tangled hair wastes a lot of effort re-checking things that are still far from where they belong.",
      "mascotDialogue": "It's insertion sort, just given permission to take big steps first. Shrink the gap enough times and it collapses into ordinary insertion sort for the final, finest pass."
    },
    "2": {
      "code": "void shellSort(int arr[], int n) {\n    for (int gap = n/2; gap > 0; gap /= 2) {\n        for (int i = gap; i < n; i++) {\n            int temp = arr[i];\n            int j = i;\n            while (j >= gap && arr[j - gap] > temp) {\n                arr[j] = arr[j - gap];\n                j -= gap;\n            }\n            arr[j] = temp;\n        }\n    }\n}",
      "highlightLines": [
        1,
        2,
        3,
        6,
        7,
        8,
        9
      ],
      "mascotDialogue": "Line 2's gap starts at n/2 and halves every outer pass. Lines 6-8 are just insertion sort's inner loop, except comparing arr[j] to arr[j - gap] instead of arr[j - 1]."
    },
