    "4": {
      "brokenCode": "void shellSort(int arr[], int n) {\n    // BUG: gap fixed at 1 instead of starting from n/2 and shrinking\n    int gap = 1;\n    for (int i = gap; i < n; i++) {\n        int temp = arr[i];\n        int j = i;\n        while (j >= gap && arr[j - gap] > temp) {\n            arr[j] = arr[j - gap];\n            j -= gap;\n        }\n        arr[j] = temp;\n    }\n}",
      "bugDescription": "Hard-coding gap = 1 with no outer shrinking loop means this function still sorts correctly (it's now identical to plain insertion sort), but it loses shell sort's entire performance advantage — the whole point of the gap sequence was to move far-apart elements quickly before falling back to fine-grained comparisons.",
      "mascotDialogue": "This is a subtle kind of bug: the output is correct, but the algorithm quietly stopped being the algorithm it claims to be. Not every bug crashes — some just quietly throw away the reason you chose this approach."
    },
    "5": {
      "challengeType": "multiple-choice",
      "question": "What does shell sort become once its gap sequence shrinks all the way down to 1?",
      "answer": "Ordinary insertion sort",
      "hints": [
        "Look at the inner loop's structure with gap=1.",
        "arr[j - gap] with gap=1 is just arr[j - 1]."
      ],
      "solution": "With gap = 1, the inner loop's comparison arr[j - gap] becomes arr[j - 1] — exactly standard insertion sort's comparison, which is why shell sort is described as a generalization of it."
    }
